# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction of this branch. rcc drops LLVM's code generator and
writes x86-64 assembly itself; the system cc assembles, links and
preprocesses. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and =docs/llvm.md=,
the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made in
=docs/design-calls.md=. LLVM stays where it benefits and is rewritten where
it has to be fought. The entries below are the slices, in order; the
chapters after this one hold the LLVM-era queue, and each says what
leaves with the pipeline.

** TODO Slice 1 of the back end: integers, control, calls

The mirror of the first slice under LLVM: the integer types, the
scalar operators, =if=, =while=, =for=, =return= and calls, as x86-64
assembly from the tree, through a code generator that owns the
registers. Every local in its slot, parameters stored at entry, the
register stack for expressions, jump code for conditions, the System V
convention at calls.

Fix: =src/c1.cc= and =src/c1.hh= beside =emit.cc=, selected by a
driver flag until the LLVM path is retired.

Check: the integer programs of =tests/corpus= give their expected
statuses through the new back end, and the integer rows of the cost
corpus are re-pinned as =rcc -S= text read against =backend.md=.

** TODO Slice 2 of the back end: pointers and strings

Pointers and =void *=, arrays as locals and parameters, subscripts as
scaled-index addressing, =&= as =lea=, pointer arithmetic and
comparison, casts, =sizeof=, string and character literals in =.data=
(G17), rip-relative addressing.

Check: the pointer programs of the corpus and the pointer rows of the
cost corpus, as for slice 1.

** TODO Slice 3 of the back end: register variables

=register= locals and parameters in the callee-saved registers, in
declaration order, a block's registers freed at its end; a register
parameter moved from its argument register at entry; saved and
restored in the prologue and epilogue. No slot, no =phi=, no join:
the variable is its register. G5 for a register variable is the
register's content, unwritten until the first write.

Check: =register-loop=, =register-compare=, =register-undef= and
=g5-register-undef= under the new back end.

** TODO Retire the LLVM pipeline

When the corpus and the cost corpus pass through the new back end:
=emit.cc= and the flag table of =main.cc= leave; =-S= writes
assembly; =-c= runs the system cc on it; =llc= is no longer run.
=cost.md= chapters 2, 4 and 5 leave with it, chapter 3 is the table
the back end was written from, =guarantees.md='s IR clauses become
instruction clauses, =shape.md= describes the new pipeline, and
=findings.md= closes with what became of its five.

Check: =make test= green with no =llc= on the machine's path.

** TODO Redo the measurement

=make bench= under the new back end. The tuned programs declare more
=register= variables than the five the discipline holds (matmul's
=main= ten, collatz seven, sieve six); the first five of each
function take the registers and the rest take slots, with a warning,
and the numbers move. The
movement is a result about the model, and the tuned sources may be
re-tuned to the set, as a PDP-11 programmer tuned to three.

Check: chapter 6 re-recorded, with the register count per program.

** TODO aarch64

A second instruction table and the AAPCS64 convention: =x0= to =x7=
for arguments, =x19= to =x28= callee-saved, ten register variables.
After x86-64 is whole.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved; under the literal pipeline the guard of G4, the
round trip of G8 and the laundering of G16 are no longer emitted,
because nothing between the source and the instruction reasons about
the program.

** TODO G7's "Lost" in the rationale misses the equality fold

=rationale.md= says G7 loses "nothing beyond G6". The corpus says
otherwise: =clang -O2= folds =&a + 1 == &b= to false for two distinct
locals, the one-past-the-end address included, where rcc compares the
addresses and finds them equal (=tests/corpus/g7-ptr-equal.c=, 1
under rcc, 0 under clang). A relational compare of the two is not
folded.

Fix: the Lost clause of G7 names the equality fold between distinct
objects, with the corpus program as its probe.

Check: the rationale's G7 names the fold and the program.

** TODO The rationale has no G17

=guarantees.md= has G17, the writable string literal, and its row in
the never-emitted table; =rationale.md= has neither, so the promise
has no recorded argument, alternatives or probe. The corpus has the
probe: =clang -O2= folds the load from a literal to its initializer
and drops the store (=tests/corpus/g17-string-store.c=, 122 under
rcc, 97 under clang).

Fix: a Storage section in the rationale with G17: the ISO paragraph,
the promise, the IR, what is lost (equal literals merged, the
read-only section, the fold of a load from a literal), and the probe.

Check: the rationale has a G17 entry and the table row.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
the literal pipeline, =llc= with FastISel and the rewriting passes
off. The entries below the first are the findings of the measurement
(=docs/findings.md=); they are facts about LLVM's code generator and
leave with it when the pipeline is retired. Under the back end of
=docs/backend.md= a multiply is =imul=, a constant is an immediate, a
register variable is a register, and a negative constant is folded in
the parser.

** TODO A function whose every path returns still gets the indeterminate slot

=needs_return_slot= allocates =%ret.indeterminate= in every non-void
function but =main=, for the value a fall off the end returns (G5).
A function whose last top-level statement is a =return= cannot fall
off the end, so its slot is a dead =alloca=, eight bytes of frame per
such function.

Fix: no slot when the body's last statement is a =return=; the
syntactic test is enough, since the slot is read only by the fall-off
path =finish= writes.

Check: =int f(void) { return 1; }= has no =ret.indeterminate= in its
IR; a function ending in an =if= without =else= still has one.

** TODO The selector rewrites a multiply and an unsigned division by a power of two

FastISel turns a =mul= by a power of two into =shl=, a =udiv= by one
into =shr= and a =urem= by one into =and= (=FastISel::fastEmit_ri_=
and =FastISel::selectBinaryOp=, =FastISel.cpp=); no option gates it.
So =x * 4= is =shl $0x2= for any =x=, and for an unsigned =x=, =x /
4= is =shr $0x2= and =x % 4= is =and $0x3=. A signed division or
remainder by a power of two stays =cltd; idiv=, as =div-four= pins.
Chapter 1 says =x / 4= is a division, and chapter 3 gives =imul= and
=div= for every operand; neither holds for these three, and no cost
row covers them. =tests/perf/collatz.c= declares its value signed so
that its halving divides.

Fix: decide between the emitter freezing a power-of-two constant
operand of =mul=, =udiv= and =urem=, as it freezes the constant of a
compare, so the instruction written is selected, and recording the
rewrite in chapter 4 as the selector's own rule, with chapters 1 and
3 qualified.

Check: cost rows for =x * 4=, unsigned =x / 4= and unsigned =x % 4=
pin =imul=, =div= and =div=, or pin the rewrite chapter 4 records.

** TODO A pass with no switch drops a constant already in its register

=machine-latecleanup=, after register allocation, removes an
instruction that loads an immediate into a register already holding
that immediate on every path to it; =llc= 23.1.3 has no option to
disable it. In =tests/perf/collatz.c=, =x % 2= loads 2 into =rcx= for
its =idiv=, and =x / 2= in the next block divides by that =rcx= with
no =mov= of its own: =llc -print-after-all= shows two =MOV32ri64 2=
up to that pass and one after it. Chapter 2 names the machine
combiner as the one pass without a switch, and chapter 1 says the
same expression written twice is computed twice.

Fix: name the pass in chapter 2 and its effect in chapter 4, with a
cost row of one constant used in two blocks, the first dominating
the second.

Check: the row's pin shows one =mov= of the constant, and chapter 2
names both passes.

** TODO Chapter 3 has no row for unary minus and complement

C has no negative constant: =-1= is unary minus applied to 1, and the
emitter writes it as =sub i32 0, 1=, then a =sext= where the type is
wider. So =long x = -1= is =xor=, =sub= and =cltq= where =long x = 2=
is one =movq $2=, and =p[-1]= negates and extends the 1 at each
access where =p[1]= is a displacement. Chapter 3 has no row for =-a=
or =~a=, and its constant row reads as if every constant costs
nothing.

Fix: a row for =-a= and =~a= that says a negative constant is a
negation at run time, with a cost program.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO A register pointer to a local array is a lea at each use

A local array's address is a frame index to the selector, and a
=register= variable initialized with it is that address itself, since
a register variable is its SSA value. The selector materializes it
with a =lea= from =rsp= at each use, inside a loop too: in a loop,
=p != first= with =register int *first = a;= is a =lea= and a =cmp=,
the same as =p != a=. Chapter 3's row for a =register= local says "a
register".

Fix: the =register= row of chapter 3 says that a register variable
holding a local's address is a =lea= at each use, with a cost
program.

Check: the row and its pin.

* Tests

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file in /tmp

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited and =core_pattern= is =/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, as on this
machine, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core of about
290 KB in =/tmp=, and =timeout= says "the monitored command dumped
core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =/tmp/core.*=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the IR
expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the IR
and leaves no file.

** TODO A failed =llc= under =-c= may leave a partial object

With =-c=, =llc= writes straight to the output path. If it fails
after opening the file, the path holds a partial object that a
later =make= would take for a good one. Unchecked.

Fix: write to a temporary beside the output and rename on success,
or confirm =llc= removes its output on failure.

Check: a fake =llc= that writes then exits 7 leaves no output file.

* Emitter

The IR emitter leaves with the LLVM pipeline. The three entries below
leave with it: the first two are properties of the IR, and the third,
the join of register variables, has no counterpart when a register
variable is a fixed register.

** TODO The object names the temporary IR file, so no two builds match

The IR has no =source_filename=, so =llc= names the module after the
file it reads, the driver's temporary =rcc-XXXXXX.ll=. The object's
file symbol is that name (=objdump -t= shows =rcc-BRXkWH.ll=), and two
=rcc -c= builds of the same source differ in it.

Fix: the emitter writes ~source_filename = "<the .c path>"~ at the
top of the IR.

Check: two =rcc -c= builds of =tests/cost/local.c= compare equal with
=cmp=, and =objdump -t= names the =.c= file.

** TODO A loop's end block with several predecessors

When =break= and =continue= arrive, a loop's end block gets several
predecessors: the two-arm join of the register variables generalizes
to n arms then.

Fix: =join= over a list of arms.

Check: a register variable assigned before a =break= has that value
after the loop.

* Parser

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO GNU extensions in system headers reach the parser as identifiers

`clang -E` of a program that includes `stdio.h` or most other glibc
headers leaves `__attribute__`, `__extension__`, `__restrict`,
`__inline`, `__asm__` and their arguments in the text; the lexer
hands them over as identifiers and the parser refuses the first.
`limits.h` has none, so the first slice does not meet them. Any
corpus program that calls into libc will. The same family: `_Bool`,
`bool`, `inline` and every keyword outside =tok_t= arrive as
identifiers and fail as undeclared names rather than as `not yet`.

Fix: decide between skipping the extensions in the parser (an
attribute and its parenthesized argument list dropped, the others
dropped as words) and preprocessing with a flag set that hides them.
A probe says which headers need what.

Check: `#include <stdio.h>` followed by an empty `main` parses.

** TODO An array size must be one integer literal

The parser takes an array size only as a lone integer token: =int
a[96 * 96];= fails with "not yet: array size other than an integer
constant", though an integer constant expression of any form makes a
fixed-size array, 6.7.7.3p4. A macro that expands to a literal works;
one that expands to an expression does not. =tests/perf/matmul.c=
writes its size out as 9216.

Fix: evaluate an integer constant expression for the size: the
integer constants and character constants, the arithmetic, shift,
bitwise, relational, logical and conditional operators on them,
casts to an integer type, and =sizeof=.

Check: =int a[96 * 96];= has 9216 elements in the IR, and =int a[n];=
with =n= a variable is still refused.

* Lexer

